Let the Matroska demuxer read on when its input moves after its end.

The Matroska demuxer sets its done flag when it meets the end of its input, and from then on
matroska_read_packet answers end of file. Only matroska_read_seek clears the flag. FFmpeg's HLS
demuxer never calls it: an HLS seek resets the byte reader under the playlist's inner demuxer and
feeds it the segment it seeks to. So once an HLS playlist of WebM or Matroska segments had been read
to its end, every later seek returned without an error and no packet ever followed. Before the end,
the same seek worked, and MP4 and MPEG-TS segments seek after the end too.

When the flag is set but the input is no longer at its end, something moved the input since, so
the demuxer clears the flag and resynchronises on the next level 1 element, as it already does
after a parse error. A file read to its end stays at its end, because nothing clears the input's
end flag but a seek.

Applied by BuildFFmpegTask before configure, after 0002. Fixes yuroyami/KiteFFmpeg#125.

diff --git a/libavformat/matroskadec.c b/libavformat/matroskadec.c
index 18849af..7ce49b8 100644
--- a/libavformat/matroskadec.c
+++ b/libavformat/matroskadec.c
@@ -4486,6 +4486,16 @@ static int matroska_read_packet(AVFormatContext *s, AVPacket *pkt)
         matroska->resync_pos = avio_tell(s->pb);
     }
 
+    /* KiteFFmpeg: the input moved after its end was reached, without matroska_read_seek, as
+     * when an HLS playlist of WebM segments seeks and hls.c resets the byte reader under this
+     * demuxer. Resynchronise on the next level 1 element instead of answering EOF for ever. */
+    if (matroska->done && !avio_feof(s->pb)) {
+        matroska->done = 0;
+        ret = matroska_resync(matroska, avio_tell(s->pb) - 1);
+        if (ret < 0)
+            return ret;
+    }
+
     while (matroska_deliver_packet(matroska, pkt)) {
         if (matroska->done)
             return (ret < 0) ? ret : AVERROR_EOF;
